        {
          "id": 2236849140,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Sure.  This is part of my work on fuzzing the wiretap library, so the only places I have looked so far are in `wiretap/`.\n\nThe first candidate is in `wiretap/capsa.c`, where function `capsa_open` seeks past the first 17 kBytes of the input file.  This is clearly necessary for compatibility with the actual file format, but it absolutely destroys any practical ability to apply the fuzzer to that file format when the fuzzer spends most of its time mutating data that the file format decoder won't even inspect.  Hiding that seek operation inside an `#ifdef FUZZING_BUILD_MODE_UNSAFE_FOR_PRODUCTION` will break file format compatibility when in fuzzing mode (and hence will limit the utility of actual capture files in seeding the fuzzer corpus), but will allow the fuzzer to actually achieve some coverage.\n\nThe second candidate is as a mechanism to inhibit the wiretap library's support for file compression... otherwise the fuzzer spends an awful lot of effort exploring all the various decompression code instead of the file formats themselves.  I haven't settled upon an effective and non-intrusive way of implementing this, but -- unless there is a foreseeable need for such an option in non-fuzzing contexts -- it is likely that it will also be hidden behind this macro.",
          "author": {
            "id": 7889756,
            "username": "dariusd0",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "dariusd0",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f0f4a609abaf984c9cfe277f1321b8db104aea7290fcbb7fb0c5749a00d32e39?s=80&d=identicon",
