        {
          "id": 2236954710,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Fuzzing capture files has two purposes:\n\n1. fuzz-testing the code that reads the files;\n2. fuzz-testing the code that dissects packet data.\n\nFor the first, the file structure is fuzzed, in ways that will probably cause the file to be unreadable (which is a good thing - it means that the code that reads the files is not trusting the file's structure to be valid, and is handling cases where it's not valid).\n\nFor the second, the file structure should mostly *not* be fuzzed; otherwise, the reader will give up when it sees a malformed file record, and any fuzzed packets following that point won't be dissected.  The only sorts of fuzzing of the file structure that should be done are things such as fuzzing time stamps in ways that will cause the file reading code to pass along the time stamps to the dissectors, possibly fuzzing the \"actual\" packet size (fuzzing the *captured* packet size should be done only by fuzzers that understand the file format and will change the amount of packet data to match the changed size), etc.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
